#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b19a236-cd8f-52be-9ed2-24581059f7f4" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Triangle Based on Its Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "7dd8ef62-3aaf-5db9-9d85-6953f0520e8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2713" :question/problem "Classify the triangle shown below.\n\n![](images/af/afafbd1dafb3e2e7849134f2dd2e71260153802bb34fb4a22dd178a81edda09d.png)" :question/worked-solution "In the triangle we see two congruent sides,\n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC},\n$$\n\nbut the third side is not congruent:\n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} \\not\\cong \\overline{BC}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since only two sides are congruent, $\\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overline{BC}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "391481e0-3bd2-570f-8948-5618d8eb40c4" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Triangle Based on Its Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "503325cd-6b72-5216-999f-6601f54aa1b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-2716" :question/problem "Given a triangle ![](images/33/335d1d0e07d6f4a7f3daaa9458a221cb5eebc714866d6494d310699b5b62d78e.svg), we know that ![](images/0d/0d097e17cb3fe4b7dbb225dcbea036db715b976ee6728b0e5709b55ce9100e51.svg) and ![](images/f6/f6ff20d28baf805ac27eb6a7822291c152a9b44e6b0743a1a7fe59635bfb5528.svg) Based on the angles, which type of triangle is ![](images/af/afa0d0e402ca98fc0a1a940db76dd0e08071e0ad436191b596e0a6fb14e69027.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) be the measure of ![](images/88/886c073571c9bd4fcf1515db8eda5f14ad06f8eb1a7b0612410eb48e7e744a2a.svg) Then ![](images/0f/0f90935af5a2c2117a80eadb5bbd88380726fd68fc830df2d81e3b3b4a99a242.svg)\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles is ![](images/47/4725d33446084ddb65f6736ea0750a68503811aff88fa69e321dee24002e95e4.svg), we have \n![](images/ca/cad788019a25c5b35243f9b4613b75863be29389a9cc71f0708e3235e3300bf2.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/4f/4f435d11ba07ac4cd7b06579abd2b5a29657db4e2b6c3ce097e9f6df0907e6fd.svg) and ![](images/a7/a77859d66a33dd7ee085d5eea33781e4aadba4d93f2c5cc99cef1e9a128d3b8e.svg) \n\nSo, ![](images/14/141e6fbb250a22f9c3e054f7bebb3a7e071a1b108418aa8275aac87fa2027b80.svg) is an obtuse triangle because it has an obtuse angle (![](images/d5/d543d4ca81776165911a27bbf1cba5e8265c99a599fa8df4e11bad00e10c4903.svg))."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3360e34f-afe6-5f9e-be39-91e2d51ba353" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Sides of a Triangle by Size Given the Interior Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "cbac3c76-7a3e-5a86-8e03-667874fe97f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2719" :question/problem "Order the sides of the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ above from the shortest to the longest.\n\n![](images/2a/2ad883fbaaa6fe0ffa87cbfc4b070f6e72ec3b4d62286ede37e3380f8fd6df25.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the missing angle $\\angle A.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+{80}^{∘}+{75}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{155}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={25}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m\\angle A <      m\\angle C <      m\\angle B.$\n\nSince $\\angle B$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overline{CA}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $\\angle A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overline{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC < BA < CA\n$$\n\nand therefore the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overline{BC}, \\overline{BA}, \\overline{CA}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115054 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "676e3d29-f6fa-5fe4-aae4-e9c0e974ee74" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Angles of a Triangle by Size Given the Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "9c478978-dbb7-53c7-b076-c763b4d89c19" :question/math-academy-id "e-4268" :question/problem "Order the angles of the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ from smallest to largest.\n\n![](images/d4/d4c8a6d27b910f674d8dc85a707e14278fe66dc02c36f4aa75cdc2211cd3bbd2.png)" :question/worked-solution "The shortest side is $\\overline{BC},$ so the smallest interior angle is $\\angle{A}.$\n\nThe longest side is $\\overline{AB},$ which means that the opposite angle, $\\angle C$, must be the largest one.\n\nTherefore, the required order must be\n\n$$\n\\angle A,\\,  \\angle B,\\,  \\angle C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "676e3d29-f6fa-5fe4-aae4-e9c0e974ee74" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Angles of a Triangle by Size Given the Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f516fb6-3127-5870-bfe6-21c7a1c8c830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠X,\\,∠Z,\\,∠Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487813} {:answer/id #uuid "90dc1a14-4702-5ea6-a6fd-58af7310de63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠X,\\,∠Y,\\,∠Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487841} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7e1bb4-2dc6-584f-813a-1cdad8b9da7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠Z,\\,∠X,\\,∠Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487927} {:answer/id #uuid "c45f1f97-9f73-5aab-80b8-7dea64c363a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠Y,\\,∠Z,\\,∠X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487939} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b4e1f1-efbe-530d-bbb5-ad3959ec153e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠Y,\\,∠X,\\,∠Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f516fb6-3127-5870-bfe6-21c7a1c8c830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠X,\\,∠Z,\\,∠Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487813} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea43407-9f1e-56af-bd97-8affc0978055" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72d7422a-3ddb-5828-b3d9-7f65634fb116" :question/math-academy-id "q-118189" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e7eb91b29c5a284d1ec7d487df5693dd5b10a18ff21dc1ef6223e47d32474e5b.png)\n\nOrder the angles of the triangle $△XYZ$ from smallest to largest." :question/worked-solution "The longest side is $\\overset{―}{XZ},$ which means that the opposite angle, $∠Y$, must be the largest one.\n\nThe shortest side is $\\overset{―}{YZ},$ so the smallest interior angle is $∠X.$\n\nTherefore, the required order must be\n\n$$\n∠X,\\,∠Z,\\,∠Y\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "391481e0-3bd2-570f-8948-5618d8eb40c4" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Triangle Based on Its Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8eb6db-0234-5a72-be2e-24ab338a1f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={90}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is a right triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487629} {:answer/id #uuid "528ef47f-3e72-58d7-9c9d-cd9c7a893107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={120}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an acute triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487731} {:answer/id #uuid "c92f9e06-ec8a-55be-a541-69741620ec05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={70}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487951} {:answer/id #uuid "e0adb8dd-6d79-55b7-8779-9fc7e836c6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={70}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an acute triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487994} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bce8e3-015d-54a4-927a-762131b47801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={120}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e8eb6db-0234-5a72-be2e-24ab338a1f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={90}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is a right triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487629} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cc75fa6-12d5-5223-9738-c28364a4f153" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78cb72f5-fa99-5a05-bb80-ea35eabe4485" :question/math-academy-id "q-27488" :question/problem "Let $△ABC$ be a triangle such that $m∠A=2m∠C$ and  $m∠B=m∠A+m∠C.$ \nWhich of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the measure of the angle $∠C,$ then $m∠A=2x$ and $m∠B=2x+x=3x.$\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles is ${180}^{∘}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+3x+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =2\\cdot {30}^{∘}={60}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠B & =3\\cdot {30}^{∘}={90}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠C & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $△ABC$ is a right triangle because it has a right angle ($m∠B={90}^{∘}$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "391481e0-3bd2-570f-8948-5618d8eb40c4" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Triangle Based on Its Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d740a44-5fdf-5115-b12e-fbcb42310ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={80}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487589} {:answer/id #uuid "4d1f7ff3-f5c1-59b7-b19f-089459911a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={30}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is a right triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487717} {:answer/id #uuid "607c8806-dc33-5802-9c08-518552228347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={110}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487760} {:answer/id #uuid "73db0680-a903-5af9-9dd2-27e97bd5941a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={90}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is a right triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487798} {:answer/id #uuid "d806c811-ffd9-55db-a30e-683106aeb5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={70}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an acute triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d806c811-ffd9-55db-a30e-683106aeb5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m∠B={70}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an acute triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487976} :answer-field/id #uuid "15e8e2bb-20e7-5195-bda0-dee07f150d04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3212a7f-a352-5c42-a46c-feb54d4245b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-27496" :question/problem "Given a triangle $△ABC$, we know that $m∠B$ is ${10}^{∘}$ less than $m∠A$ and ${40}^{∘}$ more than $m∠C.$ \n Based on the angles, which of the following statements best describes the triangle $△ABC?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the measure of $∠C.$ Then \n\nFrom the given information we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & =x+{40}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & =m∠B+{10}^{∘}=x+{50}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles is ${180}^{∘}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ (x+{50}^{∘})+(x+{40}^{∘})+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & ={30}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}={80}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠B & ={30}^{∘}+{40}^{∘}={70}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠C & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $ABC$ is an acute triangle because all three angles are acute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3360e34f-afe6-5f9e-be39-91e2d51ba353" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Sides of a Triangle by Size Given the Interior Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076bc481-a526-5ba8-8a57-a7b2f0af4833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AC}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487580} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6857f4-dd41-503e-bd0c-5f034613f857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{AC},\\overset{―}{BC}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487759} {:answer/id #uuid "663fd4ea-aa24-5d4c-84b8-2ebaa3d2b246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AC},\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{BC}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487770} {:answer/id #uuid "ee59f014-7b2f-5f34-a1f1-1a4fcbc99fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AC},\\overset{―}{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488017} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1499a7-15ed-58ad-a196-3a1befd0bcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{AC}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd1499a7-15ed-58ad-a196-3a1befd0bcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{AC}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488040} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fc96287-4f1c-5c40-8537-c5bc49b7fa84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "139c2760-4e19-5b44-bfa9-ae58e86dcfa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-52998" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0d8e956fad2f4ef75498692d353f5508247c79bdd908279119c1789ca358b8d.png)\n\nOrder the sides of the triangle $△ABC$ above from the shortest to the longest." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the missing angle $A.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+{90}^{∘}+{46}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{136}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={44}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠B$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $∠A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC<AB<AC\n$$\n\nand therefore, the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{AC}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3360e34f-afe6-5f9e-be39-91e2d51ba353" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering the Sides of a Triangle by Size Given the Interior Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f0f8eeb-281c-5023-8994-95c153cefa9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answe